{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789405848,"nonce":"wOE4DssDMYRtfLx/z0BEqt89Hvg=","title":"Python: dicts with a settled shape or settled kinds should be structs and typed tables","message":"The commonest shape of a data script is a list of dict literals with\nconstant string keys read back by those keys (`bench_records.py`), and\na dict keyed by strings or ints that is counted into (`bench_tokenize.py`,\n`totals[g] = totals.get(g, 0) + v`). Today every dict is a `List\u003cAny\u003e`\nof key, value boxes scanned linearly with `zb_any_eq`, so a field read\nis a string-compare loop and a row is a dozen allocations. records:\nzypy 1.22 s against CPython 0.19 s and PyPy 0.19 s; tokenize's counting\nstage is 0.3 s of its 0.46 s.\n\nThree tiers, decided by inference the way list element kinds are:\n\n1. A literal whose keys are all constant strings, never written with a\n   non-constant key and never iterated as a mapping, is a shape: a\n   struct with one field per key (the class path), so `row[\"score\"]`\n   is a field load. Inference records the shape per literal site and\n   joins across the writes that reach it; a `del`, a dynamic key or an\n   escape into a generic dict consumer demotes it to tier 2.\n2. Keys and values of settled kinds become `Dict\u003cK, V\u003e` with unboxed\n   storage and direct hashing and equality, built per kind pair in the\n   built-in library like `zb_list_*_\u003ckind\u003e`; the hash index 12f1439\n   asks for lands here. Mixed kinds widen to `Dict\u003cAny, Any\u003e` (the\n   list rule, 202606d). `d[k] = d.get(k, 0) + 1` lowers to one probe.\n3. `Dict\u003cAny, Any\u003e` keeps today's representation plus the hash index.\n\nThe dict release is then the typed-list release (b86f3ce's shape);\n93da585 covers the in-language hash table itself.","files":null}]}